%I A399668 #54 Nov 26 2026 14:54:46
%S A399668 0,1,2,3,5,7,9,11,13
%N A399668 a(n) is the minimum number of edges of any sum-free edge saturated graph with n vertices.
%C A399668 A sum-free edge saturated graph is a simple graph whose vertices are labeled by positive integers, with repetitions allowed, such that: (a) no vertex label is the sum of the labels of two or more distinct, adjacent vertices, and (b) adding any missing edge causes this property to fail.
%C A399668 Let a(n) be the minimum number of edges in such a graph on n vertices, minimized over all positive-integer labelings and all graphs satisfying these conditions.
%e A399668 a(4) must be less than or equal to 4 because of:
%e A399668    2-2
%e A399668    | |
%e A399668    1-1
%e A399668 but we can do better than that:
%e A399668    3-2-1-1
%e A399668 has the fewest number of edges on a saturated graph with 4 vertices so a(4) = 3.
%e A399668 Note: Those three edges cannot be increased. For example, we cannot add an edge to make this:
%e A399668    3-2
%e A399668    | |
%e A399668    1-1
%e A399668 because 3 = 2+1.
%e A399668 a(8) must be less than or equal to 11 because this graph has 11 edges, and we cannot add an edge without some vertex being the sum of its neighbors:
%e A399668    1-1
%e A399668    |/
%e A399668    1
%e A399668    |\
%e A399668    4-2
%e A399668    |/|\
%e A399668    5 3-3
%e A399668 For example, if we tried to add an edge between the "5" vertex and one of the "3" vertices, the graph would fail because the vertex "5" would be the sum of two of its neighbors: 5=2+3.
%K A399668 nonn,more,new
%O A399668 1,3
